WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.426 --> 00:00:04.083
Unit four. Simple Coding Concepts!

2
00:00:05.021 --> 00:00:09.619
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emily, your technology teacher.

3
00:00:09.669 --> 00:00:13.113
Hi, Ms. Emily! What do we learn today?

4
00:00:13.163 --> 00:00:17.172
Today we learn about Simple Coding Concepts. Let's go!

5
00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:21.695
Code. Say it with me. Code!

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.882
Instruction. Say it with me. Instruction!

7
00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:37.609
Sequence. Say it with me. Sequence!

8
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.871
Command. Say it with me. Command!

9
00:00:48.356 --> 00:00:52.023
Block. Say it with me. Block!

10
00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:59.456
Program. Say it with me. Program!

11
00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:07.443
Debug. Say it with me. Debug!

12
00:01:10.974 --> 00:01:14.508
Order. Say it with me. Order!

13
00:01:17.988 --> 00:01:33.159
Code is a set of instructions that tells a computer what to do, given in a specific order. I put together a sequence of a command or block to build a program. If something goes wrong, I debug it to fix the mistake.

14
00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:36.990
Now I understand. Thank you, Ms. Emily!

15
00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:43.362
Look! What's the word?

16
00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:44.562
Sequence!

17
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:52.041
Look! What's the word?

18
00:01:52.091 --> 00:01:53.241
Debug!

19
00:01:54.005 --> 00:02:11.208
Did you know? In 1947, engineers found a real moth stuck in an early computer and taped it into their notebook as the 'first actual case of bug being found' — though engineers already called problems 'bugs'!

20
00:02:11.258 --> 00:02:14.014
Wow! I did not know that!

21
00:02:14.718 --> 00:02:23.891
Think and answer. Why do you think the order of instructions matters in coding?

22
00:02:23.941 --> 00:02:25.091
Great answer!

23
00:02:26.029 --> 00:02:32.363
Great job today! Keep learning, and see you next time. Goodbye!

24
00:02:32.413 --> 00:02:35.469
Goodbye, everyone! See you next time!

